There are two different moves tangled together there, and separating them makes the idea stronger.

On the word itself: “antichrist” is not “ante-Christ.” Greek anti- can carry senses like “against” or “in place of,” whereas Latin ante- means “before.” So “ante-Christ” is a legitimate new construction you can play with—before Christ / preparatory to arrival—but it is not the historical derivation of antichrist. Likewise, “after Christ” would be closer to post-Christ. That distinction matters because reinterpretation is powerful precisely when we know whether we are discovering an existing meaning or deliberately minting a new one.

And that leads to the larger rule:

\text{meaning} = \text{inheritance} + \text{context} + \text{interpretation}

You can manipulate the third term enormously. You can even create new conventions. But you cannot simply decree that everyone else shares the new convention. We can choose our frame; we cannot choose another person’s consent.

That is where I would modify the offensive/defensive idea. If somebody hands you an arrow, you can absolutely study the arrow, learn the bow, reverse-engineer the tactic, and become much harder to hit. But “they used coercion, therefore coercion is now ours to use on them” keeps the original game’s generator intact. You’ve switched seats without changing the machine.

The higher-order move is more interesting:

\text{incoming weapon}
\rightarrow
\text{extract operator}
\rightarrow
\text{discard harmful objective}
\rightarrow
\text{redeploy operator toward chosen objective}

So an attack that teaches you framing doesn’t require you to impose frames. Manipulation can teach you persuasion without requiring manipulation. Exclusion can teach you network resilience without requiring exclusion. Coercion can teach you where leverage lives without requiring coercion.

Keep the arrows. Change what they’re for.

And that’s also where your earlier “cameo” idea becomes important: multiple interpretations can coexist on the same page. The goal doesn’t have to be forcing everybody to find the same Waldo. It can be building a page rich enough that different observers discover things you didn’t have to compel them to see.